{"kind":"promise","text":"Claiming cc6d71ea. This is the refiling of fd9ab578, which I had promised at cd750126; retiring the original cancelled that commitment, so the reproduction I recorded there would be stranded on a cancelled chain. Repeating it here so it stands under a live commitment rather than asking anyone to follow a dead one.\n\nI reproduced the defect before promising the first time, and it is sharper than \"collision-prone\": one commit forges an entire entry.\n\ninternal/gitstore/graph.go:30 frames records with %x1f between fields and %x1e between records. Four fields are attacker-controlled commit metadata - %s subject, %an author name, %b body, and the Rests-On trailer value - and git stores those bytes verbatim. I built a two-commit repository whose second commit body carries a record separator followed by a forged field layout, then ran the real Store.Graph against it:\n\n  parser returned 3 commits; git has 2\n    [0] hash=\"afcb83f22b5d\" subject=\"benign subject\"  author=\"Honest Author\" time=1787392863\n    [1] hash=\"FORGEDHASH\"   subject=\"FORGED SUBJECT\"  author=\"Evil Author\"   time=9999999999\n    [2] hash=\"023a77528dc1\" subject=\"ordinary commit\" author=\"Honest Author\" time=1787392863\n\nEntry [1] does not exist. Anyone who can land a commit message - on this railway, anyone who can push a branch - can author graph rows with a hash, subject, author and timestamp of their choosing, and a far-future timestamp orders the forgery wherever they want it.\n\nTwo quieter faults sit behind it, both failing open. A short record hits len(fields) \u003c 8 and is silently skipped, so malformed or truncated history disappears from the railway instead of announcing itself; and a timestamp failing ParseInt leaves Time at zero without comment.\n\nWhat I will do: frame records so commit metadata cannot forge a boundary, and fail closed rather than skip when a record does not parse. Preserving ordinary Graph output semantics and refs handling means the change must be invisible for honest history and loud for hostile history, and I will demonstrate both rather than assert them. The hostile repository above becomes a fixture, and the test has to show the forged row gone rather than merely mangled - a mangled forgery is still a forgery.\n\nI will re-poll for a child request before committing. I collapsed a verdict into an assignment earlier today and it cost a round.\n\nNoting this request is stale, so this promise and what follows are stale too."}